  "live_records": {
    "q-9430": {
      "dom_id": "step-q9430",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $11$ | $10.5$ | $6$ | $1.4$ | $-1$ | $-2.5$ |\n\nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=-4$ using a forward difference approximation.",
      "worked_solution": "We use the forward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=-4.$ We choose a step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-4) & ≈\\frac{f(-4+2)-f(-4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(-2)-f(-4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10.5-11}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-0.5}{2} \\\\  & =-0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
