      "source_example_id": "e-152",
      "knowledge_point": "Estimating a Derivative From a Graph Using a Forward Difference Approximation",
      "kp_id": "e-152"
    },
    "q-342030": {
      "dom_id": "step-q342030",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190413/assets/75be1bf675a2a967bc95326fcf06e94437c107cad89fb2cf76ce4d1cc0939838.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=1$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${g}^{′}(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=1.$ Applying the above formula with $x=1$ and $h=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{g(1+1)-g(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(2)-g(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-4}{1} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
