    "q-47384": {
      "dom_id": "step-q47384",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190413/assets/3a7d2d87e38f4c4660503e46a3fd3a43b2ddb677f56093880140c3bd321091f9.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=4.$ Applying the above formula with $x=4$ and $h=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{g(4+2)-g(4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(6)-g(4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-4}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
