    "q-47379": {
      "dom_id": "step-q47379",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "The population of a city is given by the function $n(t),$ where $t$ is the time, in years, from when records first began. The values of $n(t)$, in thousands, for different values of $t$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $n(t)$ | $851$ | $856$ | $861$ | $865$ | $870$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of growth in population in the second year $(t=2)$ using a forward difference approximation.",
      "worked_solution": "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{n}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{n(t+h)-n(t)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $t=2.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{n(2+1)-n(2)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{n(3)-n(2)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{865-861}{1} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at $t=2$ the rate of growth is approximately $4\\,000$ people per year.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
