    "q-342044": {
      "dom_id": "step-q342044",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "The electrical energy level of a battery powering a house was recorded over time for $10$ hours, starting from time $t=0$ hours. The following table shows the energy level $E(t)$, in kilowatt-hours, recorded at time $t.$\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $E(t)$ | $40$ | $34$ | $29$ | $24$ | $18$ | $12$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change of energy level at time $t=2$ using a forward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of change is negative if the electrical energy level decreases.\n\nAt $t=2,$ the estimated rate of change is {{field-1}} kilowatt-hours per hour.",
      "worked_solution": "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{E}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{E(t+h)-E(t)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $t=2.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{E(2+2)-E(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{E(4)-E(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{29-34}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{2} \\\\  & =-2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, at $t=2$ the rate of change was approximately $-2.5$ kilowatt-hours per hour.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
