      "source_example_id": "e-154",
      "knowledge_point": "Estimating a Derivative From a Graph Using a Forward Difference Approximation: Word Problem",
      "kp_id": "e-154"
    },
    "q-342078": {
      "dom_id": "step-q342078",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190413/assets/d49d6cb5160d4a5edba3c3d973885584f4f9a2fa2fd9c036ed8dffb1cbbc09e0.png)\n\nA balloon is deflating over time. The graph of the volume $V(t)$, in cubic centimeters, after $t$ seconds is shown above. Approximate the rate of change of the volume at $t=15$ using a forward difference with step size $h=20.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of change is negative if the volume decreases.\n\nAfter $15$ seconds, the rate of change of the volume is {{field-1}} cubic centimeters per second.",
      "worked_solution": "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{V(t+h)-V(t)}{h},\n$$\n\nat the time $t=15$ with step size $h=20.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(15) & ≈\\frac{V(15+20)-V(15)}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{V(35)-V(15)}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1000-1500}{20} \\\\  & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after $15$ seconds, the rate of change of the volume is $-25$ cubic centimeters per second.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
